## Customize flags given to the VM: https://www.erlang.org/doc/apps/erts/erl_cmd.html

## Do not set -name or -sname here. Prefer configuring them at runtime
## Configure -setcookie in the mix.exs release section or at runtime

## Number of dirty schedulers doing IO work (file, sockets, and others)
##+SDio 5

## Increase number of concurrent ports/sockets
##+Q 65536

## Give memory back to the operating system.
##
## This board holds 363.9 MB. A browse of a library of 53,105 items measured 276 MB
## of live data, which a collection later brought back to 141 MB, and a resident size
## that stayed at 293 MB. A second browse then finds little free.
##
## The default of `fullsweep_after` is 65535, which means that a process almost never
## collects its old heap. A process that held one page of a list therefore keeps it
## for the life of the device. 10 makes each process give that memory up.
##
## Carrier options such as `+MBacul` are not here on purpose. A measurement on this
## version of the runtime showed `acul` at 0 for each allocator whatever the option
## said, so those options do nothing that this file can prove. A flag that stops the
## runtime from starting stops the device from booting.
-env ERL_FULLSWEEP_AFTER 10

## Hold the memory of the runtime down, on a board where Linux sees 363.9 MB.
##
## **`R` is the driver allocator, and `D` is `std_alloc`.** Every guide that names
## `+MD` for the driver gets this wrong, and such a flag tunes the wrong allocator and
## reports nothing. A measurement of `:erlang.system_info({:allocator, :driver_alloc})`
## is the way to know that a flag arrived.
##
## `aobf` is address order best fit. It fills the carrier with the lowest address
## first, so the carriers at the top empty and the runtime gives them back. The
## default, `aoffcbf`, spreads the blocks and holds more carriers than it needs.
##
## `smbcs` and `lmbcs` are the size of a carrier, in KB. The default asks the operating
## system for 256 KB and 5 MB at a time, which is a lot of rounding on a board of this
## size. 64 KB and 128 KB waste less.
+MRas aobf
+MRsmbcs 64
+MRlmbcs 128
+MBas aobf
+MBsmbcs 64
+MBlmbcs 128
+MHas aobf
+MHsmbcs 64
+MHlmbcs 128

## The first carrier of the runtime, in KB.
+MMscs 20

## **`acul` is absent on purpose.** A guide will tell you that `+MBacul 0` stops the
## runtime from holding an empty carrier. It does the opposite: 0 turns the abandonment
## of a carrier off, and 0 is the default already. A measurement on OTP 29 showed that
## no value of it arrives at all, so the flag is a comment with no effect.
##
## `+t` is absent as well. The atom table costs about 2 MB, and a build that passes the
## limit of it stops. That is a poor trade.

## How many processes and ports the runtime holds room for. The tables are made at the
## start, whatever runs later. This device held 795 processes and 24 ports, and the
## defaults are 262,144 and 65,536: the process table alone measured 12 MB.
+P 50000
+Q 8192

## Use Ctrl-C to interrupt the current shell rather than invoking the emulator's
## break handler and possibly exiting the VM.
+Bc

## Require an initialization handshake within 10 minutes from the startup guard
## See https://nerves-runtime.hexdocs.pm/readme.html#assisted-firmware-validation-and-automatic-revert
-env HEART_INIT_TIMEOUT 600

# Allow time warps so that the Erlang system time can more closely match the
# OS system time.
+C multi_time_warp

## Choose whether to load all code at system startup
## See https://www.erlang.org/doc/system/system_principles.html#code-loading-strategy
## Slow devices sometimes benefit from choosing embedded mode
##-mode interactive

# Load code as per the boot script since not using archives
# See https://www.erlang.org/doc/man/init.html#command-line-flags
-code_path_choice strict

## Disable scheduler busy wait to reduce idle CPU usage and avoid delaying
## other OS processes. See https://www.erlang.org/doc/apps/erts/erl_cmd.html#+sbwt
+sbwt none
+sbwtdcpu none
+sbwtdio none

## Save the shell history between reboots
## See https://www.erlang.org/doc/apps/kernel/kernel_app for additional options
-kernel shell_history enabled

## Enable heartbeat monitoring of the Erlang runtime system
-heart -env HEART_BEAT_TIMEOUT 30

## Start the Elixir shell

-noshell
-user elixir
-run elixir start_cli

## Enable colors in the shell
-elixir ansi_enabled true

## Options added after -extra are interpreted as plain arguments and can be
## retrieved using :init.get_plain_arguments(). Options before the "--" are
## interpreted by Elixir and anything afterwards is left around for other IEx
## and user applications.
-extra --no-halt
--
--dot-iex /etc/iex.exs
